byte ranges, in order) laid out again, each on its own !! at the column where it starts. `text` is formatted apart from those statements, and they do !! not depend on the lines around them (see [[stands_alone]]), so this gives what formatting the !! whole text again would, in a fraction of the time. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: text !! the text, after lines were split integer , intent ( in ) :: changed (:, :) !! byte ranges of the statements to lay out type ( format_options_t ), intent ( in ) :: opts !! the formatting options character ( len = :), allocatable :: out type ( format_options_t ) :: alone type ( diagnostic_t ), allocatable :: unused (:) type ( string_builder_t ) :: buf character ( len = :), allocatable :: piece integer :: j , next alone = opts next = 1 do j = 1 , size ( changed , 2 ) associate ( a => changed ( 1 , j ), b => changed ( 2 , j )) call buf % append ( text ( next : a - 1 )) alone % base_indent = verify ( text ( a : b ) // 'x' , ' ' ) - 1 ! its indentation call format_pass ( text ( a : b ), alone , piece , unused ) call buf % append ( piece ) next = b + 1 end associate end do call buf % append ( text ( next :)) out = buf % get () end function relayout","tags":"","url":"proc/relayout.html"},{"title":"same_code – fformat","text":"public  function same_code(a, b, reordered_use, a_tokens) True if a and b have the same code tokens, in the same order, with the same text and the\nsame joins across line breaks (blanks, comments and line layout may differ). Names and dot\noperators are compared ignoring case, as Fortran does, and the two spellings of a relational\noperator ( < and .lt. ) are the same. With reordered_use , use statements may also be in a different order within a run of them\n(not across other statements or preprocessor lines), and so may the items of an only: list. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: a first source text character(len=*), intent(in) :: b second source text logical, inte