tion->proc~first_non_blank proc~emit_continuation->proc~fitting_column proc~emit_continuation->proc~input_column proc~emit_continuation->proc~put proc~first_piece_width first_piece_width proc~fitting_column->proc~first_piece_width proc~line_width line_width proc~fitting_column->proc~line_width proc~input_column->proc~utf8_width proc~put->proc~builder_append proc~put->proc~utf8_width proc~first_piece_width->proc~utf8_width proc~first_piece_width->proc~line_pieces proc~line_width->proc~utf8_width proc~line_width->proc~line_pieces Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Called by proc~~format_text~~CalledByGraph proc~format_text format_text proc~process_text process_text proc~process_text->proc~format_text proc~process_file process_file proc~process_file->proc~process_text proc~process_stdin process_stdin proc~process_stdin->proc~process_text proc~run run proc~run->proc~process_file proc~run->proc~process_stdin Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Source Code subroutine format_text ( src , opts , out , stat , errmsg , diags ) !! Format `src`. On success `out` is the formatted text and `stat` is `FMT_OK`. !! !! As a safety check, the code tokens of the result are compared with those of the input. If they !! differ, which would be a bug in the formatter, `out` is the unchanged input and `stat` is !! `FMT_ERR_INTERNAL`. character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: src !! source text to format type ( format_options_t ), intent ( in ) :: opts !! formatting options character ( len = :), allocatable , intent ( out ) :: out !! the formatted text integer , intent ( out ) :: stat !! `FMT_OK` or `FMT_ERR_INTERNAL` character ( len = :), allocatable , intent ( out ) :: errmsg !! error message; unallocated if `stat` is `FMT_OK` type ( diagnostic_t ), allocatable , intent ( out ), optional :: diags (:) !! problems found while formatting ! each pass splits each long line once, and a statement gets at most MAX_CONTINUATIONS ! continuation lines, so this many passes split every line that can be split integer , parameter :: MAX_PASSES = MAX_CONTINUATIONS + 1 type ( diagnostic_t ), allocatable :: d (:), unused (:) type ( token_list_t ) :: tokens integer , allocatable :: long (:), changed (:, :) character ( len = :), allocatable :: prepared , split integer :: k , nsplit ! a sorted `only:` list is written on one line for splitting, or broken to fit if splitting is off prepared = convert_relational ( sort_use ( src , opts % sort_use , opts % sort_use_only , & merge ( 0 , opts % line_length , opts % split_lines )), opts % relational ) call format_pass ( prepared , opts , out , d , tokens ) ! no code longer than the limit, as most files: nothing to split, and no need to look for ! code lines that are too long if ( opts % split_lines . and . opts % line_length > 0 . and . too_long ( out , opts % line_length )) then ! split long lines and format again, until every line fits or nothing more can be split; only ! the statements that were split need laying out again, unless one of them depends on the ! lines around it do k = 1 , MAX_PASSES split = split_long_lines ( out , opts % line_length , opts % continuation_indent , nsplit , changed ) if ( nsplit == 0 ) exit if ( allocated ( changed ) . and . opts % comment_spacing < 0 ) then out = relayout ( split , changed , opts ) else call format_pass ( split , opts , out , unused ) end if if (. not . too_long ( out , opts % line_length )) exit end do if ( too_long ( out , opts % line_length )) then long = long_code_lines ( out , opts % line_length ) do k = 1 , size ( long ) d = [ d , diagnostic_t ( long ( k ), 'line ' // to_s ( long ( k )) // ' of the formatted file is longer than ' // & to_s ( opts % line_length ) // ' characters and cannot be split' )] end do end if end if if ( opts % comment_spacing >= 0 ) then ! comments are kept where they line up in the input; once the code is formatted, some of ! them may no longer line up, so decide again on the result (which is then stable) split = out call format_pass ( split , opts , out , unused ) end if d = [ limit_diagnostics ( src ), d ] if ( present ( diags )) diags = d stat = FMT_OK ! the tokens of the first pass are those of `src` if sorting and relational spelling changed nothing if ( len ( prepared ) /= len ( src ) . or . prepared /= src ) call tokenize ( src , tokens ) if (. not . same_code ( src , out , opts % sort_use . or . opts % sort_use_only , tokens )) then stat = FMT_ERR_INTERNAL errmsg = 'internal error: formatting would change the code, so the file was left unchanged' out = src end if end subroutine format_text","tags":"","url":"proc/format_text.html"},{"title":"empty – fformat","text":"private  subroutine empty(cfg) A configuration with nothing set. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( config_t ), intent(out) :: cfg set to have no file, no settings and no [files] lists Called by proc~~empty~~CalledByGraph proc~empty empty proc~find_config find_config proc~find_config->proc~empty proc~load_config load_config proc~find_config->proc~load_config proc~load_config->proc~empty proc~config_for config_for proc~config_for->proc~find_config proc~config_for->proc~load_config proc~directory_files directory_files proc~directory_files->proc~config_for proc~options_for options_for proc~options_for->proc~config_for proc~process_text process_text proc~process_text->proc~options_for proc~run run proc~run->proc~directory_files proc~process_file process_file proc~run->proc~process_file proc~process_stdin process_stdin proc~run->proc~process_stdin proc~process_file->proc~process_text proc~process_stdin->proc~process_text Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Source Code subroutine empty ( cfg ) !! A configuration with nothing set. type ( config_t ), intent ( out ) :: cfg !! set to have no file, no settings and no `[files]` lists cfg % path = '' allocate ( cfg % settings ( 0 )) end subroutine empty","tags":"","url":"proc/empty.html"},{"title":"find_config – fformat","text":"public  subroutine find_config(dir, cfg, stat, errmsg) The configuration for files in directory dir (an absolute path): from the nearest config file\nin dir or its parents. cfg%path is '' if there is none. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: dir absolute path of the directory type( config_t ), intent(out) :: cfg the configuration found integer, intent(out) :: stat nonzero if the config file found cannot be read character(len=:), in