nt(in) :: first first token of the part of the line to measure integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one Return Value integer Calls proc~~first_piece_width~~CallsGraph proc~first_piece_width first_piece_width proc~line_pieces line_pieces proc~first_piece_width->proc~line_pieces proc~utf8_width utf8_width proc~first_piece_width->proc~utf8_width Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Called by proc~~first_piece_width~~CalledByGraph proc~first_piece_width first_piece_width proc~fitting_column fitting_column proc~fitting_column->proc~first_piece_width proc~comment_column comment_column proc~comment_column->proc~fitting_column proc~emit_continuation emit_continuation proc~emit_continuation->proc~fitting_column proc~emit_statement emit_statement proc~emit_statement->proc~fitting_column proc~emit_statement->proc~comment_column proc~emit_statement->proc~emit_continuation proc~emit_comment_line emit_comment_line proc~emit_comment_line->proc~comment_column proc~layout layout proc~layout->proc~emit_statement proc~layout->proc~emit_comment_line proc~format_pass format_pass proc~format_pass->proc~layout proc~format_text format_text proc~format_text->proc~format_pass proc~relayout relayout proc~format_text->proc~relayout proc~relayout->proc~format_pass proc~process_text process_text proc~process_text->proc~format_text proc~process_file process_file proc~process_file->proc~process_text proc~process_stdin process_stdin proc~process_stdin->proc~process_text proc~run run proc~run->proc~process_file proc~run->proc~process_stdin Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Source Code integer function first_piece_width ( w , src , tokens , first , lend ) result ( width ) !! Width of tokens `first:lend` up to the end of the first code token that has another code token !! after it on the line (the shortest first part of a split), or a huge value if there is none. type ( writer_t ), intent ( in ) :: w !! the output and the alignment state character ( len =* ), intent ( in ) :: src !! the source text type ( token_list_t ), intent ( in ) :: tokens !! its tokens integer , intent ( in ) :: first !! first token of the part of the line to measure integer , intent ( in ) :: lend !! last token of the line, its line break if it has one type ( piece_t ), allocatable :: pieces (:) integer :: k , m , total width = huge ( 1 ) / 2 call line_pieces ( w , src , tokens , first , lend , pieces ) total = 0 do k = 1 , size ( pieces ) associate ( text => src ( pieces ( k )% first : pieces ( k )% last )) if ( pieces ( k )% blanks > 0 ) then total = total + pieces ( k )% blanks else if ( scan ( text , achar ( 10 ) // achar ( 13 )) > 0 ) return total = total + utf8_width ( text ) end if end associate if ( pieces ( k )% tok == 0 ) cycle if (. not . is_code_kind ( tokens % tok ( pieces ( k )% tok )% kind )) cycle if ( pieces ( k )% tok == first . and . tokens % tok ( first )% kind == TK_INT ) cycle ! a statement label do m = k + 1 , size ( pieces ) if ( pieces ( m )% tok == 0 ) cycle if ( is_code_kind ( tokens % tok ( pieces ( m )% tok )% kind ) . and . . not . tokens % tok ( pieces ( m )% tok )% glued ) then width = total return end if exit end do end do end function first_piece_width","tags":"","url":"proc/first_piece_width.html"},{"title":"fitting_column – fformat","text":"private  function fitting_column(w, src, tokens, f, lend, candidates, from, prefix, always_original) result(col) The first of the candidates columns at which the output line fits within the line length, or\nthe smallest if none fits. The last candidate is the line's original column: if only that one\nfits, the line is recorded in long_lines . When lines are split ( w%keep_original false), the\noriginal column is not a candidate for code, since splitting will make the line fit; it is\nstill used if always_original is true (for comments, which are not split). Also when lines are\nsplit, a candidate other than the last is accepted if the line can be split to fit there: if\nits first piece up to a possible break fits. The input line starts with token f . The output line is prefix characters (e.g. & for a\nleading continuation marker) followed by tokens from:lend (default f:lend ). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: f first non-blank token of the input line integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one integer, intent(in) :: candidates (:) columns to try, best first; negative ones are skipped integer, intent(in), optional :: from first token written after the prefix; requires prefix integer, intent(in), optional :: prefix width of the text written before token from logical, intent(in), optional :: always_original try the original column even when lines are split Return Value integer Calls proc~~fitting_column~~CallsGraph proc~fitting_column fitting_column proc~first_piece_width first_piece_width proc~fitting_column->proc~first_piece_width proc~line_width line_width proc~fitting_column->proc~line_width proc~line_pieces line_pieces proc~first_piece_width->proc~line_pieces proc~utf8_width utf8_width proc~first_piece_width->proc~utf8_width proc~line_width->proc~line_pieces proc~line_width->proc~utf8_width Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Called by proc~~fitting_column~~CalledByGraph proc~fitting_column fitting_column proc~comment_column comment_column proc~comment_column->proc~fitting_column proc~emit_continuation emit_continuation proc~emit_continuation->proc~fitting_column proc~emit_statement emit_statement proc~emit_statement->proc~fitting_column proc~emit_statement->proc~comment_column proc~emit_statement->proc~emit_continuation proc~emit_comment_line emit_comment_line proc~emit_comment_line->proc~comment_column proc~layout layout proc~layout->proc~emit_statement proc~layout->proc~emit_comment_line proc~format_pass format_pass proc~format_pass->proc~layout proc~format_text format_text proc~format_text->proc~format_pass proc~relayout relayout proc~format_text->proc~relayout proc~relayout->proc~format_pass proc~process_text process_text proc~process_text->proc~format_text proc~process_file process_file proc~process_file->proc~process_text proc~process_stdin process_stdin proc~process_stdin->proc~process_text 