ny comment, blank or preprocessor lines before it do i = lend + 1 if ( i > tokens % n ) exit lend = line_end_token ( tokens , i ) if ( line_kind ( tokens , i , lend ) == SK_CODE ) exit end do if ( i > tokens % n ) then lend = tokens % n exit end if end do call stmts % push ( statement_t ( SK_CODE , start , lend )) i = lend + 1 end do end subroutine split_statements","tags":"","url":"proc/split_statements.html"},{"title":"ffmt_io – fformat","text":"Reading and writing whole files as bytes, so that line endings are preserved on every platform. Used by module~~ffmt_io~~UsedByGraph module~ffmt_io ffmt_io module~ffmt_cli ffmt_cli module~ffmt_cli->module~ffmt_io Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Subroutines public  subroutine read_file (path, text, stat, errmsg) All bytes of file path . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: path the file to read character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: text the contents; '' if the file cannot be opened integer, intent(out) :: stat iostat of the open or read: nonzero on failure character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: errmsg the system's message, if stat is nonzero public  subroutine read_stdin (text) All of standard input. Lines are read as text, so each line ends with a line feed, except a\nlast line without one; carriage returns of CRLF line endings are kept as part of the line. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: text the input public  subroutine write_file (path, text, stat, errmsg) Replace the contents of file path (created if needed) with the bytes of text . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: path the file to write character(len=*), intent(in) :: text the new contents integer, intent(out) :: stat iostat of the open or write: nonzero on failure character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: errmsg the system's message, if stat is nonzero","tags":"","url":"module/ffmt_io.html"},{"title":"ffmt_case – fformat","text":"Letter case of keywords, dot operators, intrinsic procedures, the constants of the intrinsic\nmodules, and kinds. Fortran names are not reserved, so a word is only treated as a keyword where its position makes it\none: the leading words of a statement ( end subroutine , integer , if , else if , select case , implicit none , ...), the attributes of a declaration before :: , in / out / inout in intent() , kind= / len= in a type, then , only: , result() and bind() . A word that names something\n( call open(...) , subroutine real , end function do ) keeps its case. Changing the case of a name\nnever changes the meaning of the code, so a misclassified word is a matter of style only. Intrinsic procedures are recognized where they are called (followed by ( , or after call ), and the\nconstants and types of iso_fortran_env and iso_c_binding wherever they are used, but not as the\nfirst word of a statement, after % , as an entity being declared, or as the name of a procedure\nbeing defined. Kinds are the values of kind= , the kind of a declaration's type ( real(dp) :: x ),\nthe kind of a literal ( 2_int64 , .true._lk , ck_'a' ) and exponent letters ( 1.0e-3 ). Uses ffmt_source ffmt_statements ffmt_options ffmt_tokens module~~ffmt_case~~UsesGraph module~ffmt_case ffmt_case module~ffmt_options ffmt_options module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_source ffmt_source module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_statements ffmt_statements module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_tokens ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_source->mo