the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public, parameter :: CASE_KEEP = 0 leave the case as it is integer, public, parameter :: CASE_LOWER = 1 lowercase integer, public, parameter :: CASE_UPPER = 2 uppercase integer, public, parameter :: CONT_ALIGN = 1 align continuation lines with the first item after the\ninnermost open bracket, or after the first = integer, public, parameter :: CONT_INDENT = 2 indent continuation lines by a fixed amount integer, public, parameter :: REL_KEEP = 0 leave the spelling as it is integer, public, parameter :: REL_LETTERS = 2 < becomes .lt. , ... integer, public, parameter :: REL_SYMBOLS = 1 .lt. becomes < , ... integer, public, parameter :: WS_ADD = 1 at least one blank on each side integer, public, parameter :: WS_KEEP = 0 leave the blanks as they are integer, public, parameter :: WS_REMOVE = 2 no blanks Derived Types type, public :: case_options_t Letter case of keywords, dot operators and logical constants, intrinsic procedures, the constants\nof the intrinsic modules, and kinds. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: constants = CASE_KEEP constants and types of iso_fortran_env and iso_c_binding : output_unit , int64 , c_int , c_ptr integer, public :: keywords = CASE_KEEP if , end do , integer , intent(in) , ... integer, public :: operators = CASE_KEEP .and. , .eq. , .true. , ... integer, public :: procedures = CASE_KEEP intrinsic procedures where they are called: size(a) , call cpu_time(t) integer, public :: types = CASE_KEEP suffixes 2_int64 and prefixes ck_'a' , exponent letters 1.0e-3 type, public :: format_options_t All options that control formatting. The defaults give 4-space indentation with the bodies\nof modules and programs not indented, and aligned continuation lines. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: base_indent = 0 columns added to the indentation of every line, so that even\nprogram units are indented (4 for code that starts in column 5) integer, public :: comment_spacing = -1 blanks before a trailing comment, or -1 to keep them; comments\naligned with one on the line before or after are kept integer, public :: continuation = CONT_ALIGN layout of continuation lines ( CONT_ALIGN or CONT_INDENT ) integer, public :: continuation_indent = 4 extra indentation of continuation lines, used with CONT_INDENT and when there is nothing to align with integer, public :: indent = 4 spaces per indentation level logical, public :: indent_comments = .true. re-indent comment lines like the code around them logical, public :: indent_fypp = .false. indent the code inside fypp blocks ( #:if , #:for , #:def , ...),\nand the fypp directive lines with the code around them logical, public :: indent_procedures = .true. indent the bodies of subroutines and functions logical, public :: indent_units = .false. indent the bodies of modules, submodules and main programs logical, public :: keep_column1_comments = .false. leave comment lines that start in column 1 there (with indent_comments ) type( case_options_t ), public :: letter_case letter case of keywords and operators integer, public :: line_length = 132 maximum line length (0 for no limit). Alignment that would make a\nline longer falls back to a smaller indentation. integer, public :: relational = REL_KEEP spelling of relational operators: REL_KEEP , REL_SYMBOLS ( .lt. becomes < ) or REL_LETTERS ( < becomes .lt. ) logical, public :: sort_use = .false. sort the use statements of each group by module name logical, public :: sort_use_only = .false. sort the names in only: lists type( whitespace_options_t ), public :: spaces whitespace rules within lines logical, public :: split_lines = .true. split code lines that are longer than line_length type, public :: whitespace_options_t Modes of the whitespace rules. The defaults follow the most common style in practice where it is\nclear-cut, and keep the rest. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: assignment = WS_ADD = outside brackets: assignments, initializations, do i = 1, n integer, public :: comma = WS_KEEP , : with WS_ADD , no blank before and at least one after integer, public :: concat = WS_REMOVE // integer, public :: conditional = WS_ADD ? and : of a conditional expression (c ? a : b) (F2023) integer, public :: control_paren = WS_ADD between if , while , select case , where , ... and ( , and\nbetween ) and a final then integer, public :: declarations = WS_ADD :: integer, public :: defined_operators = WS_KEEP binary user-defined operators: a .plus. b integer, public :: keyword_args = WS_REMOVE = inside brackets: f(x=1) , intent= , len=* integer, public :: logical = WS_KEEP .and. .or. .eqv. .neqv. integer, public :: mult_div = WS_KEEP binary * / integer, public :: percent = WS_REMOVE % integer, public :: plus_minus = WS_KEEP binary + - integer, public :: pointer = WS_ADD => : pointer assignment, renames, associate integer, public :: power = WS_REMOVE ** integer, public :: relational = WS_KEEP == /= < <= > >= and .eq. etc. integer, public :: slice_colon = WS_REMOVE : inside brackets: a(1:n)","tags":"","url":"module/ffmt_options.html"},{"title":"ffmt_linebreak – fformat","text":"Splitting code lines that are longer than the line length. A line is split at a token boundary, preferring (in this order) after a comma, after an operator, = or :: , after an opening bracket, and anywhere between two tokens. Within the best kind of\nbreak that fits, the rightmost is used. The first part ends with & and the rest goes on a new\nline; the layout pass then aligns it. Only the code counts: a trailing comment may extend past\nthe limit and moves with the last part. Lines are not split inside tokens (strings in particular),\nin protected code, in comment or preprocessor lines, or when a token spans lines. Uses ffmt_source ffmt_statements ffmt_strings ffmt_tokens module~~ffmt_linebreak~~UsesGraph module~ffmt_linebreak ffmt_linebreak module~ffmt_source ffmt_source module~ffmt_linebreak->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_statements ffmt_statements module~ffmt_linebreak->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_strings ffmt_strings module~ffmt_linebreak->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_tokens ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_linebreak->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_directives ffmt_directives module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_directives module~ffmt_lexer ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_tokens->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_tokens Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~ffmt_linebreak~~UsedByGraph module~ffmt_linebreak ffmt_linebreak module~ffmt_format ffmt_format module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_linebreak module~ffmt_cli ffmt_cli module~ffmt_cli->module~ffmt_format Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public, parameter :: MAX_CONTINUATIONS = 255 continuation lines a statement may have before\nFortran 2023 (F2018 6.3.2.1); splitting stops there Functions private  function break_point (tokens, src, a, lend, start_col, line_length) result(best) The token after which to split tokens a:lend of a line that starts at token a in column start_col , or 0 if the code fits or cannot be split. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens the tokens of src character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text integer, intent(in) :: a first token of the line integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one integer, intent(in) :: start_col column of token a integer, intent(in) :: line_length maximum line width, in characters Return Value integer public  function long_code_lines (src, line_length) result(lines) Line numbers of code lines whose code (without a trailing comment) is longer than line_length ,\noutside protected code. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text integer, intent(in) :: line_length maximum line width, in characters; 0 or less for no limit Return Value integer, allocatable, (:) private pure function priority (text, kind) result(prio) How good a place the end of a token with this text is for a line break (1 is best). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text lowercase text of the token integer, intent(in) :: kind token kind ( TK_* ) Return Value integer public  function split_long_lines (src, line_length, indent, nsplit, changed) result(out) src with each code line longer than line_length split once. The new continuation lines are indented\nby indent more than the line they come from if it is the first line of a statement, and like\nit if it is a continuation line, which the layout pass will usually leave in place. nsplit returns the number of splits. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text integer, intent(in) :: line_length maximum line width, in characters integer, intent(in) :: indent indentation step, in blanks integer, intent(out) :: nsplit number of lines split integer, intent(out), optional, allocatable :: changed (:,:) where the statements that were split\nare in out : bytes changed(1, j):changed(2, j) .\nNot allocated if one of them cannot be laid out\nagain on its own (see stands_alone ) Return Value character(len=:), allocatable private pure function stands_alone (source, k) True if statement k of source , a code statement, can be laid out again on its own after\none of its lines is split, giving what laying out the whole text would: it takes up whole\nlines (no ; before or after it on its lines, no byte order mark), has no statement label\n(which stays at the start of the line), and is not followed by a comment line that may be\nlined up with a comment at the end of its last line. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( source_t ), intent(in) :: source the source integer, intent(in) :: k index into source%stmts Return Value logical","tags":"","url":"module/ffmt_linebreak.html"},{"title":"ffmt_structure – fformat","text":"Block structure of a file: the nesting depth of every statement, from a stack of open\nconstructs, plus diagnostics for statements that do not fit (an end with nothing to close,\nan else outside an if , a construct still open at the end of the file, ...). Uses ffmt_tokens ffmt_statements ffmt_constructs ffmt_source ffmt_strings module~~ffmt_structure~~UsesGraph module~ffmt_structure ffmt_structure module~ffmt_constructs ffmt_constructs module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_constructs module~ffmt_source ffmt_source module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_statements ffmt_statements module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_strings ffmt_strings module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_tokens ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_directives ffmt_directives module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_directives module~ffmt_lexer ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_tokens->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_tokens Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~ffmt_structure~~UsedByGraph module~ffmt_structure ffmt_structure module~ffmt_format ffmt_format module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_structure module~ffmt_layout ffmt_layout module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_layout module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_structure module~ffmt_cli ffmt_cli module~ffmt_cli->module~ffmt_format Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private, parameter :: FY_CLOSE = 3 #:endif , #:endfor , ... integer, private, parameter :: FY_MIDDLE = 2 #:elif , #:else , #:nextarg integer, private, parameter :: FY_OPEN = 1 #:if , #:for , #:def , #:block , #:call , #:mute integer, private, parameter :: FY_OTHER = 0 not a block directive ( #:set , $: , cpp, ...) integer, private, parameter :: PP_ELSE = 2 integer, private, parameter :: PP_ENDIF = 3 integer, private, parameter :: PP_IF = 1 integer, private, parameter :: PP_OTHER = 0 Derived Types type, public :: diagnostic_t A problem with the block structure, such as an end that matches no open construct. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: line = 0 source line of the statement concerned character(len=:), public, allocatable :: message what is wrong, e.g. 'if is not closed' type, private :: saved_stack_t Scope stacks for one preprocessor conditional: as it was at the #if (restored at each #elif / #else ) and as it was at the end of the first branch (used after #endif ). Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial type( scope_t ), public, allocatable :: after_first (:) the stack at the end of the first branch; unallocated until then type( scope_t ), public, allocatable :: at_if (:) the stack at the #if type, private :: scope_stack_t The open constructs, outermost first. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial type( scope_t ), public, allocatable :: s (:) storage; only s(1:top) is in use integer, public :: top = 0 number of open constructs Type-Bound Procedures procedure, public :: push => stack_push open a construct procedure, public :: restore => stack_restore replace the open constructs by saved ones procedure, public :: snapshot => stack_snapshot the open constructs, to save type, private :: scope_t An open construct on the scope stack. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: construct = C_NONE one of the C_* constants integer, public :: do_label = 0 for do 10 ... : the label of the statement that ends the loop, else 0 integer, public :: line = 0 source line of the opening statement type, public :: structure_t The block structure of a source: for each item (see ffmt_statements ) what it is and how\ndeeply it is nested. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public, allocatable :: depth (:) nesting depth of each item: the constructs around it integer, public, allocatable :: fypp (:) fypp blocks ( #:if , #:for , #:def , ...) around each\nitem; a directive line is outside its own block type( stmt_info_t ), public, allocatable :: info (:) classification of each item ( ROLE_NONE for non-code) integer, public, allocatable :: procedures (:) how many are procedure definitions (subroutine,\nfunction or separate module procedure, but not\ninterface bodies) integer, public, allocatable :: units (:) how many of those are program units (module, submodule\nor main program) Functions private pure function closes (closing, open) True if an end of kind closing ends the open construct open . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: closing construct named by the end ( C_ANY for a bare end ) integer, intent(in) :: open construct that is open Return Value logical private pure function conditional_kind (text) result(kind) Kind of a preprocessor line (lowercase text): PP_IF , PP_ELSE ( #elif , #else ), PP_ENDIF or PP_OTHER . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text text of the preprocessor token, in lowercase Return Value integer private  function fypp_depths (source) result(fypp) The number of fypp blocks around each item of source . The directives are counted wherever\nthey are, also inside a continued statement; a directive line itself is at the level outside\nits block, like else and end statements. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( source_t ), intent(in) :: source the source Return Value integer, allocatable, (:) private pure function fypp_kind (text) result(kind) Kind of a preprocessor line (lowercase text) as a fypp block directive: one of the FY_*\nconstants. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text text of the preprocessor token, in lowercase Return Value integer private pure function middle_fits (middle, open) True if a middle statement of construct middle ( else of C_IF , contains , ...) may\nappear directly inside the open construct open . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name integer, intent(in) :: middle construct of the middle statement integer, intent(in) :: open construct that is open Return Value logical private pure function stack_snapshot (self) result(scopes) The open constructs, outermost first. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( scope_stack_t ), intent(in) :: self the stack Return Value type( scope_t ), allocatable, (:) Subroutines public  subroutine analyze_structure (source, views, st, diags) Classify every statement and compute its depth: the number of constructs enclosing it, and\nhow many of those are program units and procedures, so callers can choose not to indent\ntheir bodies.\nAn opening statement is at the depth outside its construct, the statements inside are one\ndeeper, and middle ( else , contains , ...) and closing statements are at the opening depth.\nNon-code items get the depth of the code around them. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( source_t ), intent(in) :: source the source type( statement_view_t ), intent(in) :: views (:) the view of each item (see statement_views ) type( structure_t ), intent(out) :: st the structure type( diagnostic_t ), intent(out), allocatable :: diags (:) structure problems found, in the order found private pure subroutine stack_push (self, sc) Open construct sc , growing the storage as needed. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( scope_stack_t ), intent(inout) :: self the stack type( scope_t ), intent(in) :: sc the construct to open private pure subroutine stack_restore (self, scopes) Replace the open constructs by scopes (outermost first). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name class( scope_stack_t ), intent(inout) :: self the stack type( scope_t ), intent(in) :: scopes (:) a saved stack (see stack_snapshot )","tags":"","url":"module/ffmt_structure.html"},{"title":"ffmt_format – fformat","text":"Formatting a whole source text: the main library entry point. Uses ffmt_structure ffmt_options ffmt_tokens ffmt_lexer ffmt_statements ffmt_relational ffmt_whitespace ffmt_use_sort ffmt_layout ffmt_source ffmt_strings ffmt_linebreak ffmt_case module~~ffmt_format~~UsesGraph module~ffmt_format ffmt_format module~ffmt_case ffmt_case module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_case module~ffmt_layout ffmt_layout module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_layout module~ffmt_lexer ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_linebreak ffmt_linebreak module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_linebreak module~ffmt_options ffmt_options module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_relational ffmt_relational module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_relational module~ffmt_source ffmt_source module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_statements ffmt_statements module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_strings ffmt_strings module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_structure ffmt_structure module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_structure module~ffmt_tokens ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_use_sort ffmt_use_sort module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_use_sort module~ffmt_whitespace ffmt_whitespace module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_whitespace module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_case module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_structure module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_whitespace module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_linebreak->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_linebreak->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_linebreak->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_linebreak->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_relational->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_relational->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_relational->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_relational->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_relational->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_directives ffmt_directives module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_directives module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_constructs ffmt_constructs module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_constructs module~ffmt_tokens->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_use_sort->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_use_sort->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_use_sort->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_use_sort->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_tokens Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~ffmt_format~~UsedByGraph module~ffmt_format ffmt_format module~ffmt_cli ffmt_cli module~ffmt_cli->module~ffmt_format Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public, parameter :: FMT_ERR_INTERNAL = 1 formatting would have changed the code; the text is returned unchanged integer, public, parameter :: FMT_OK = 0 formatted Functions private  function code_signature (src) result(sig) The code tokens of src as one string that same_code compares when use statements may\nbe reordered: for each token its kind (operators all alike), whether it is glued to the\nprevious token, and its text as key gives it. The signatures of use statements in a run\nare sorted, and so are those of the items of each only: list. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: src source text Return Value character(len=:), allocatable private pure function folded_length (text, kind) result(n) How much of a token's text, from its start, Fortran reads ignoring case: all of a name, an\noperator or a number (exponent letters and kinds), the kind prefix of a string, and none of\nanything else. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text the token's text integer, intent(in) :: kind its kind ( TK_* ) Return Value integer private  function limit_diagnostics (src) result(diags) Warnings for input beyond the limits of the Fortran 2023 standard (6.3.2.1): lines longer than\n10,000 characters, and statements of more than 1,000,000 characters (counted without comments\nand line breaks). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: src source text Return Value type( diagnostic_t ), allocatable, (:) public pure function longest_code (text) result(width) An upper bound of the width of the longest code line of text : the characters of each line\nbefore a comment, without the blanks before it, as long_code_lines counts them.\nPreprocessor lines do not count. Strings are followed across continuation lines, so that a ! inside one does not end the code, but the scan does not tokenize, so it may count more\nthan a token-based count would; never less. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text the formatted text Return Value integer private pure function longest_line (text) result(width) Width of the longest line of text , in characters (as utf8_width counts them). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text text whose lines end with LF Return Value integer private  function relayout (text, changed, opts) result(out) text with the statements at changed (byte ranges, in order) laid out again, each on its own\nat the column where it starts. text is formatted apart from those statements, and they do\nnot depend on the lines around them (see stands_alone ), so this gives what formatting the\nwhole text again would, in a fraction of the time. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text the text, after lines were split integer, intent(in) :: changed (:,:) byte ranges of the statements to lay out type( format_options_t ), intent(in) :: opts the formatting options Return Value character(len=:), allocatable public  function same_code (a, b, reordered_use, a_tokens) True if a and b have the same code tokens, in the same order, with the same text and the\nsame joins across line breaks (blanks, comments and line layout may differ). Names and dot\noperators are compared ignoring case, as Fortran does, and the two spellings of a relational\noperator ( < and .lt. ) are the same. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: a first source text character(len=*), intent(in) :: b second source text logical, intent(in), optional :: reordered_use allow reordered use statements; default false type( token_list_t ), intent(in), optional :: a_tokens the tokens of a , if already known Return Value logical private pure function same_token (sa, s, sb, t) True if code token s of sa matches code token t of sb , as their keys in code_signature would: same kind (operators all alike), same glue, and the same text,\nignoring case where Fortran does (see folded_length ), with relational operators in their\nsymbol spelling. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: sa source text of s type( token_t ), intent(in) :: s a code token of sa character(len=*), intent(in) :: sb source text of t type( token_t ), intent(in) :: t a code token of sb Return Value logical private pure function too_long (text, limit) True if a code line of text may be longer than limit characters (see longest_code ).\nA line has no more characters than bytes, so the scan is only needed when a line has more\nbytes than that, which finding the line breaks shows quickly. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text the formatted text integer, intent(in) :: limit the line length Return Value logical Subroutines private  subroutine format_pass (src, opts, out, diags, src_tokens) One pass of the formatter: structure, whitespace, case and layout. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: src source text type( format_options_t ), intent(in) :: opts formatting options character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: out the formatted text type( diagnostic_t ), intent(out), allocatable :: diags (:) problems found in this pass type( token_list_t ), intent(out), optional :: src_tokens the tokens of src public  subroutine format_text (src, opts, out, stat, errmsg, diags) Format src . On success out is the formatted text and stat is FMT_OK . Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: src source text to format type( format_options_t ), intent(in) :: opts formatting options character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: out the formatted text integer, intent(out) :: stat FMT_OK or FMT_ERR_INTERNAL character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: errmsg error message; unallocated if stat is FMT_OK type( diagnostic_t ), intent(out), optional, allocatable :: diags (:) problems found while formatting","tags":"","url":"module/ffmt_format.html"},{"title":"ffmt_config – fformat","text":"Configuration files. Settings (see ffmt_settings ) are read from the nearest of these, searching from a file's\ndirectory upward: .fformat.toml fpm.toml , in an [extra.fformat] table Settings are written as in the table of ffmt_settings , with spaces and case as tables: indent = 3 line-length = 100 [spaces] comma = \"add\" [case] keywords = \"lower\" [files] extensions = [ \"f90\" , \"F90\" ] exclude = [ \"build\" , \"legacy/*.f90\" ] The [files] table controls which files are formatted when a directory is given. Uses tomlf_constants tomlf ffmt_settings ffmt_strings ffmt_fs module~~ffmt_config~~UsesGraph module~ffmt_config ffmt_config module~ffmt_fs ffmt_fs module~ffmt_config->module~ffmt_fs module~ffmt_settings ffmt_settings module~ffmt_config->module~ffmt_settings module~ffmt_strings ffmt_strings module~ffmt_config->module~ffmt_strings tomlf tomlf module~ffmt_config->tomlf tomlf_constants tomlf_constants module~ffmt_config->tomlf_constants iso_c_binding iso_c_binding module~ffmt_fs->iso_c_binding module~ffmt_settings->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_options ffmt_options module~ffmt_settings->module~ffmt_options Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~ffmt_config~~UsedByGraph module~ffmt_config ffmt_config module~ffmt_cli ffmt_cli module~ffmt_cli->module~ffmt_config Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial character(len=*), private, parameter :: CONFIG_NAME = '.fformat.toml' Derived Types type, public :: config_t The settings of one configuration file. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial type( string_t ), public, allocatable :: exclude (:) files.exclude , likewise type( string_t ), public, allocatable :: extensions (:) files.extensions ; not allocated if the file does not set it character(len=:), public, allocatable :: path the file, or '' if there is none type( setting_t ), public, allocatable :: settings (:) the formatting settings, in the order they are applied Subroutines private  subroutine empty (cfg) A configuration with nothing set. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( config_t ), intent(out) :: cfg set to have no file, no settings and no [files] lists public  subroutine find_config (dir, cfg, stat, errmsg) The configuration for files in directory dir (an absolute path): from the nearest config file\nin dir or its parents. cfg%path is '' if there is none. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: dir absolute path of the directory type( config_t ), intent(out) :: cfg the configuration found integer, intent(out) :: stat nonzero if the config file found cannot be read character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: errmsg what is wrong, with the file name, if stat is nonzero public  subroutine load_config (path, fpm, cfg, found, stat, errmsg) Read the configuration in file path : the whole file, or with fpm its [extra.fformat] table. found is false if an fpm.toml has no such table. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: path the TOML file logical, intent(in) :: fpm path is an fpm.toml type( config_t ), intent(out) :: cfg the configuration; empty if not found logical, intent(out) :: found the file has a configuration integer, intent(out) :: stat nonzero for bad TOML, an unknown key or a bad value character(len=:), intent(out), allocatable :: errmsg what is wrong, with the file name, if stat is nonzero","tags":"","url":"module/ffmt_config.html"},{"title":"ffmt_layout – fformat","text":"Writes the formatted text of a tokenized file: statements indented by their nesting depth,\ncontinuation lines aligned or indented by a fixed amount, comment lines indented like the code\naround them, and within each line the blanks and letter case that compute_gaps and compute_case ask for. Trailing blanks are removed. Items protected by formatter directives (see ffmt_directives ) are written as they are. Uses ffmt_structure ffmt_tokens ffmt_statements ffmt_whitespace ffmt_source ffmt_case ffmt_strings ffmt_options module~~ffmt_layout~~UsesGraph module~ffmt_layout ffmt_layout module~ffmt_case ffmt_case module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_case module~ffmt_options ffmt_options module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_source ffmt_source module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_statements ffmt_statements module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_strings ffmt_strings module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_structure ffmt_structure module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_structure module~ffmt_tokens ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_whitespace ffmt_whitespace module~ffmt_layout->module~ffmt_whitespace module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_case->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_directives ffmt_directives module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_directives module~ffmt_lexer ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_constructs ffmt_constructs module~ffmt_structure->module~ffmt_constructs module~ffmt_tokens->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_options module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_source module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_whitespace->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_constructs->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_tokens Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Used by module~~ffmt_layout~~UsedByGraph module~ffmt_layout ffmt_layout module~ffmt_format ffmt_format module~ffmt_format->module~ffmt_layout module~ffmt_cli ffmt_cli module~ffmt_cli->module~ffmt_format Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended from. Dashed arrows point from a module or program unit to \nmodules which it uses. Variables Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, private, parameter :: ANCHOR_ASSIGN = 1 integer, private, parameter :: ANCHOR_DECL = 3 integer, private, parameter :: ANCHOR_ONLY = 2 Derived Types type, private :: piece_t A piece of an output line: a token, or the blanks before one. Its text is src(first:last) (with the token's letter case applied), or blanks blanks if that is positive; it is not\ncopied, so that measuring a line allocates nothing. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: blanks = 0 if positive, the piece is this many blanks instead integer, public :: first = 1 first byte of the text in the source integer, public :: last = 0 last byte of the text in the source integer, public :: tok = 0 token index, or 0 for blanks type, private :: writer_t Output being built, and the alignment state of the current statement. Components Type Visibility Attributes Name Initial integer, public :: anchor_col (3) = -1 column of the first item after an assignment = / => ,\na use statement's only: and a declaration's :: , or -1 integer, public, allocatable :: bracket_first (:) column of the first item after each open bracket, or -1;\nonly bracket_first(1:nbrackets) is in use type( string_builder_t ), public :: buf the output text type( case_marks_t ), public :: cases letter case wanted for each token (see compute_case ) integer, public :: col = 0 output column: characters since the last line break integer, public :: comment_in = -1 input column of the comment on the previous line, or -1 integer, public :: comment_out = -1 output column of that comment logical, public :: declaration = .false. a top-level :: has been seen in the current statement integer, public, allocatable :: gap (:) blanks wanted before each token (see compute_gaps ) logical, public :: generic = .false. the current statement is a generic binding logical, public :: keep_original = .true. fall back to a code line's original indentation when the\nnew one would make it too long (when lines are not split) logical, public :: line_comment = .false. a comment has been written on the current line integer, public :: line_length = 0 maximum line length, or 0 integer, public, allocatable :: long_lines (:) input lines that kept their indentation to stay within the line length integer, public :: nbrackets = 0 open brackets in the current statement integer, public :: pending_anchor = 0 the next code token is the first item after this anchor logical, public :: pending_bracket = .false. the next code token is the first item after a bracket integer, public :: prev = 0 the previous code token of the statement (index into the\ntoken list), or 0 logical, public :: separated = .false. a top-level , or : has been seen in the statement integer, public :: stmt_in = -1 input column of the last statement, while comment lines\ndirectly follow it, or -1 integer, public :: stmt_out = -1 output column of that statement Functions private  function comment_column (w, src, tokens, j, default) result(col) Output column for the comment-only line with comment token j : lined up with the comment on\nthe line above if it was lined up with it in the input, otherwise default . Records the\ncomment for the next line. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: j the comment token integer, intent(in) :: default column to use if it is not lined up with the comment above Return Value integer private pure function continuation_target (w, ind, opts) result(target) Column for the code of a continuation line: the first item after the innermost open\nbracket; outside brackets, the first item after an assignment, a use statement's only: or a declaration's :: ; otherwise a fixed indent. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(in) :: w the output and the alignment state integer, intent(in) :: ind indentation of the statement, in blanks type( format_options_t ), intent(in) :: opts the formatting options Return Value integer private pure function first_non_blank (tokens, first, last) result(f) First token in first:last that is not a blank, or last + 1 . Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens the token list integer, intent(in) :: first the token range to search integer, intent(in) :: last the token range to search Return Value integer private  function first_piece_width (w, src, tokens, first, lend) result(width) Width of tokens first:lend up to the end of the first code token that has another code token\nafter it on the line (the shortest first part of a split), or a huge value if there is none. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(in) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: first first token of the part of the line to measure integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one Return Value integer private  function fitting_column (w, src, tokens, f, lend, candidates, from, prefix, always_original) result(col) The first of the candidates columns at which the output line fits within the line length, or\nthe smallest if none fits. The last candidate is the line's original column: if only that one\nfits, the line is recorded in long_lines . When lines are split ( w%keep_original false), the\noriginal column is not a candidate for code, since splitting will make the line fit; it is\nstill used if always_original is true (for comments, which are not split). Also when lines are\nsplit, a candidate other than the last is accepted if the line can be split to fit there: if\nits first piece up to a possible break fits. Read more… Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: f first non-blank token of the input line integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one integer, intent(in) :: candidates (:) columns to try, best first; negative ones are skipped integer, intent(in), optional :: from first token written after the prefix; requires prefix integer, intent(in), optional :: prefix width of the text written before token from logical, intent(in), optional :: always_original try the original column even when lines are split Return Value integer private pure function input_column (src, t) result(col) Column of token t in the input (characters before it on its line). Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_t ), intent(in) :: t a token of src Return Value integer public  function layout (source, st, gap, cases, opts, long_lines) result(out) The formatted text of source , from its structure ( analyze_structure ), the gaps\n( compute_gaps ) and the letter case ( compute_case ) wanted. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( source_t ), intent(in) :: source the source type( structure_t ), intent(in) :: st its block structure integer, intent(in) :: gap (:) blanks wanted before each token type( case_marks_t ), intent(in) :: cases letter case wanted for each token type( format_options_t ), intent(in) :: opts the formatting options integer, intent(out), optional, allocatable :: long_lines (:) input lines that kept their indentation\nbecause the new one would make them longer\nthan opts%line_length Return Value character(len=:), allocatable private  function line_width (w, src, tokens, first, lend) result(width) Width of tokens first:lend as they will be written on one line, without the line break and\nwithout a trailing comment (compilers only limit the length of the code). A comment on a line\nof its own counts. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(in) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: first first token of the part of the line to measure integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one Return Value integer private pure function to_case (text, upper) result(t) text in ASCII upper or lower case. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text the text to convert logical, intent(in) :: upper true for upper case, false for lower case Return Value character(len=len) private pure function trim_blanks (text) result(t) text without trailing blanks and tabs. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name character(len=*), intent(in) :: text the text to trim Return Value character(len=:), allocatable Subroutines private  subroutine emit_blank (w, src, tokens, s) A blank line: only its line break (and a byte order mark) is kept. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens type( statement_t ), intent(in) :: s the blank line item private  subroutine emit_body (w, src, tokens, first, lend) Tokens first:lend of a line (see line_pieces ), while tracking open brackets and the\nanchors for aligning continuation lines. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: first first token to write integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one private  subroutine emit_comment_line (w, src, tokens, s, ind, opts) A comment line, re-indented to ind . A comment that lines up with the comment on the line\nabove (a continued trailing comment) stays lined up with it. Comment lines directly after a\nstatement that are indented more than it keep their extra indentation. With keep_column1_comments , a\ncomment that starts in column 1 stays there. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens type( statement_t ), intent(in) :: s the comment line item integer, intent(in) :: ind indentation of the code around it, in blanks type( format_options_t ), intent(in) :: opts the formatting options private  subroutine emit_continuation (w, src, tokens, f, lend, aligned, indented) A continuation line whose first non-blank token is f , with its code starting at the column aligned , or at indented or its original column if that would make the line too long. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: f first non-blank token of the line integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one integer, intent(in) :: aligned preferred column for the code (see continuation_target ) integer, intent(in) :: indented column for the code with a fixed continuation indent private  subroutine emit_fypp_line (w, src, tokens, s, ind) A preprocessor line: a fypp directive ( #: , #! , $: , @: ) is indented by ind ; a cpp line\nis written as it is. Continuation lines of the directive are not changed. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens type( statement_t ), intent(in) :: s the line, a preprocessor item integer, intent(in) :: ind indentation, in blanks private  subroutine emit_range (w, src, tokens, first, last) Append tokens unchanged. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: first the tokens first:last to append integer, intent(in) :: last the tokens first:last to append private  subroutine emit_statement (w, src, tokens, s, labeled, ind, opts) A code statement, with its continuation lines and the comment, blank and preprocessor lines\nbetween them. The first line is indented to ind ; a statement label stays at the start of\nthe line. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens type( statement_t ), intent(in) :: s the code statement logical, intent(in) :: labeled the statement has a statement label integer, intent(in) :: ind indentation of the statement, in blanks type( format_options_t ), intent(in) :: opts the formatting options private  subroutine line_pieces (w, src, tokens, first, lend, pieces) The pieces of output for tokens first:lend of a line: the tokens, with the blanks between them\nadjusted by the whitespace rules and trailing blanks removed. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(in) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: src the source text type( token_list_t ), intent(in) :: tokens its tokens integer, intent(in) :: first first token of the part of the line integer, intent(in) :: lend last token of the line, its line break if it has one type( piece_t ), intent(out), allocatable :: pieces (:) the pieces, in order private  subroutine put (w, text) Append text and track the output column. Arguments Type Intent Optional Attributes Name type( writer_t ), intent(inout) :: w the output and the alignment state character(len=*), intent(in) :: text text to append; it may contain line breaks","tags":"","url":"module/ffmt_layout.html"},{"title":"ffmt_source – fformat","text":"A source text read for formatting: its tokens, its statements and which of them formatter\ndirectives protect. Every pass that works on the text (formatting, line splitting, relational\nspelling, use sorting) starts from one of these. Uses ffmt_lexer ffmt_statements ffmt_directives ffmt_tokens module~~ffmt_source~~UsesGraph module~ffmt_source ffmt_source module~ffmt_directives ffmt_directives module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_directives module~ffmt_lexer ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_lexer module~ffmt_statements ffmt_statements module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_tokens ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_source->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_statements module~ffmt_directives->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_strings ffmt_strings module~ffmt_lexer->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_tokens module~ffmt_statements->module~ffmt_strings module~ffmt_tokens->module~ffmt_strings Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Module Module Submodule Submodule Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a submodule to the (sub)module which it is\ndescended